{
	"$schemaNote": "v2 of the Vibes contract. A variant states a palette; primal/theme/tokenMap.ts states how that palette becomes a colour theme. Nothing reads this file at runtime - src/vs/workbench/contrib/primalVibes mirrors the ids and labels by hand, and must stay in sync.",
	"$seedIsFlat": "Every key of `seed` and `surfaces` is a slot id in tokenMap.ts, spelled identically. There is no name mapping: seed.ansiBrightCyan fills slot \"ansiBrightCyan\".",
	"$semanticsAreAbsent": "error, warning, info, added, deleted, modified and conflict are DELIBERATELY not in a seed. The owner is colour blind (amber reads as green), so these must be separated by LIGHTNESS, not hue. A seeded hex cannot promise that; a synthesised one can be measured. The generator derives them from the palette and proves the separation, and the colour-blindness gate re-checks every generated theme. Do not add them here. The values the six shipping themes use are pinned as expected output in primal/theme/tokenMap.test-fixture.json.",
	"$surfacesArePinned": "`surfaces` holds the chrome planes a palette corpus never supplies (elevated, hover, input, tab, control border, muted/disabled/subtle ink, bright accent). All six vibes pin all of them, because these were eyeballed when the themes were hand-written and NO mix of seed colours reproduces them exactly. tokenMap.ts carries approximate fallbacks, with their measured error, for palettes that omit them.",
	"version": 2,
	"colorThemeExtension": "theme-primal",
	"productIconTheme": "primal-icons",
	"depthBands": {
		"$comment": "Descriptive band for how far the chrome plane sits off the editor plane, measured as |HSL lightness difference| between seed.editorBg and seed.chromeBg. The seed pins the actual planes; depth only picks the fallback strength for unpinned surfaces.",
		"soft": "< 3.75",
		"medium": "3.75 - 4.75",
		"hard": "> 4.75"
	},
